The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the fallback logic does not check if the MPTCP socket (`ssk`) is in a shutdown state before calling `tcp_shutdown()`. If the MPTCP socket is already shut down, this can lead to the TCP connection remaining open even after all pending data has been spooled, creating a race condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (READ_ONCE(msk->snd_data_fin_enable) && !(ssk->sk_shutdown & SEND_SHUTDOWN)) {
    ssk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN;
    tcp_shutdown(ssk, SEND_SHUTDOWN);
}
```
Reason:
The fix explicitly checks if the socket is not in a shutdown state before proceeding with the `tcp_shutdown()` call. By ensuring that the shutdown state is updated (`ssk->sk_shutdown |= SEND_SHUTDOWN`) before calling `tcp_shutdown()`, it prevents the TCP connection from remaining open when it should be closed, thus addressing the race condition.

